tension on the streets of myanmar this  week as the country braces for a  possible political crisis  we're not saying the military will take  power but we're not saying it won't  either  this was the moment that it all began in  a press conference on tuesday  military spokesperson zormin tun refused  to rule out the possibility of a coup  the following day military chief minang  lang stoked fears when he said the  constitution should be repealed if it  was not abided by  the comments follow weeks of complaints  from the military of voter fraud in  november's election  and demands for an investigation the  military is forcing the uec and the  government that hey no no  you can't just ignore us you know we are  the stakeholder  we have obligations  uh we are the guardian to the  constitution so  so this is a major message the military  is trying to send it to the  government and also the international  communities  fear escalated after reports of an  increasing security presence  and sightings of armoured vehicles being  moved around various cities  as tensions reached their peak on  saturday the military issued a new  statement saying the  commander-in-chief's comments had been  misinterpreted  but just hours later emboldened by  rumors hundreds of protesters gathered  in yangon at a pro-military rally  where speeches welcomed a coup with  support of the people  protests like this have been happening  on an almost daily basis over the last  few weeks  while they have been gaining attention  they failed to draw the numbers  for what would be considered a show of  popular support  but this crowd is gaining confidence  and it's stoking fear in the community  as the pressure builds there have been  violent attacks on nld supporters  watching on the nld say  accusations of voter fraud are just  being used as an excuse to sow fear  senior general minong lang is making the  nld scared and making the people scared  his main intention is to extend his  senior general position  military chief minang lang is due to  retire in july and may be fearful of  losing power  although imminent action from the  military now appears less likely tension  remains high as relations between the  civilian government and the military  reach an all-time low ali fal al jazeera  yangon
 

